\section{Where the pieces go}{

The second-difference penalty \code{S} is eigendecomposed and the basis is
split the way \code{\link[mgcv:smooth2random]{mgcv::smooth2random()}} splits \code{s()}: the null space of
\code{S} joins the fixed coefficients (\code{beta}, or \code{betad} for a
distributional parameter), and the range space becomes one
random-effect block with a single variance in \code{theta}. The smoothing
parameter is that variance's inverse, so smoothness is estimated
jointly with every other variance component rather than chosen.

\code{\link[=VarCorr]{VarCorr()}} and \code{\link[=ranef]{ranef()}} report the block under the term's own label.
Its standard deviation is a smoothing parameter, not a subject
effect.
}

\section{The knots are frozen}{

The knot vector is built once, from the range of \code{expr} evaluated on
the model frame with every nonlinear parameter set to zero, padded by
\code{pad}. It is then frozen on the frame and reused on \code{newdata}, which
is \code{\link[=poly]{poly()}}'s rule and for the same reason: a basis rebuilt from new
data is a different basis, and the coefficients would not mean what
they were fitted to mean.

Boundary knots are DISTINCT and lie outside the padded range. The
divided-difference form degenerates on the repeated boundary knots
\code{splines::splineDesign()} normally uses, so \code{ps()} places them apart
rather than exposing the choice.

This is NOT the rule of D'Alessandro, Thoresen and Sorensen (2026),
section 2.3.1, and \code{ps()} is not a reimplementation of it. That paper
rescales the spline argument to \verb{[0, 1]} between bounds
\verb{min(t) - 3 sigma_b} and \verb{max(t) + 3 sigma_b}, which are smooth
functions of the CURRENT variance estimates, so its knots move with
the fit. \code{ps()} freezes instead, for two reasons. Moving knots make
the penalty matrix \code{S} and its eigendecomposition functions of the
parameters, so the split into fixed and random coefficients would
have to be redone inside the objective; and a basis that is rebuilt
between the fit and the prediction is a different basis, which is
the rule \code{poly()} follows and the reason \verb{pad =} exists. On the
paper's own application the two agree closely enough that six of its
seven reported parameters come back at the published precision, but
they are related constructions rather than one.
}

\section{Accuracy}{

The basis agrees with \code{\link[splines:splineDesign]{splines::splineDesign()}} to 3.1e-13 absolute at
\code{k = 12} and 1.8e-11 at \code{k = 40}, with an AD input giving the same
values bit for bit and the taped derivative matching
\code{splineDesign(derivs = 1)} to 4.5e-13 and 4.5e-11 respectively. The
error grows like \code{k^(degree - 1)} because a divided difference of
truncated powers cancels terms of order \code{(range / spacing)^degree}.
\code{k} above 50 is refused for that reason; a curve that needs more than
50 basis functions needs a different basis, not a looser tolerance.
}

\section{What the rest of the package does with it}{

\code{predict()} and \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} work: the body is re-evaluated at the new
data or at the drawn coefficients, and the frozen basis is evaluated
at whatever argument comes out.

\code{predict(se.fit = TRUE)} stays refused for a nonlinear predictor.
\code{\link[=frm_lp_basis]{frm_lp_basis()}} is the route: it tapes the body and returns
\verb{d eta / d coef} as a Jacobian, which is what a delta method over a
warped curve needs.

\code{REML = TRUE}, \code{quadrature = TRUE}, \code{frmtmb_control(profile = TRUE)}
and multivariate (\code{\link[=mvbf]{mvbf()}}) models are refused by name. The first
three integrate out something a \code{ps()} block has already put in the
Laplace approximation; the fourth is untested.

The importance correction (\code{\link[=frm]{frm()}}'s \verb{importance =}) is refused, and
not by anything \code{ps()} declares: core refuses the correction for
EVERY nonlinear predictor, because a body mixes parameter values with
raw data columns and the corrected objective evaluates the predictor
once per draw.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
n_id <- 40
d <- expand.grid(t = seq(0, 1, length.out = 8), id = factor(1:n_id))
sh <- stats::rnorm(n_id, 0, 0.05)[d$id]
d$y <- 2 + sin(2 * pi * (d$t + sh)) + stats::rnorm(nrow(d), 0, 0.1)
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ lev + ps(t + shift, k = 8),
              lev ~ 1, shift ~ 0 + (1 | id), nl = TRUE),
           data = d)
fixef(fit)
}
